export const PRIVACY = {
  "html": "<p>Latchet (&quot;the app&quot;, &quot;we&quot;) helps you track spending, detect subscriptions,\nand manage business expenses. This policy explains what data we handle and how.</p>\n<p>The page you are reading on Latchet&#39;s website is generated from the same\ncopy of this policy that is kept with the app&#39;s code, so the two cannot\ndisagree.</p>\n<h2 id=\"data-we-collect-and-why\">Data we collect and why</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Data</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Account info (name, email)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You, at sign-up</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Authentication, account recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Transactions &amp; budgets</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You, or your linked bank</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">The app&#39;s core budgeting features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Accounts, settings, custom categories, tracked subscriptions</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Synced with your account so a reinstall or a second phone restores them. Each account&#39;s copy on a phone is a separate database, removed from the phone when you sign out.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Bank account data</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">Plaid (only if you link a bank)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Automatic transaction sync. We never see or store your bank credentials; Plaid provides read-only access tokens, which we store encrypted (AES-256-GCM).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Details from billing emails</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">Your Gmail or Outlook inbox, only if you connect it; or emails you forward to your private Latchet address</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Detecting subscriptions, free trials and price changes. <strong>We keep only the details we extract — merchant, the sender&#39;s domain (e.g. <code>netflix.com</code>), amount, currency, billing cycle, dates, and what kind of email it was. Never the email body, its attachments, the subject line as written, or any email address in it.</strong> See &quot;Reading your inbox&quot; below. Deleted after 90 days, or straight away when you disconnect the inbox.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Inbox connection</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You, when you connect Gmail or Outlook</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">The address of the inbox you connected, the access token Google or Microsoft issues to us, stored encrypted (AES-256-GCM), and the sync status we show you (when it last synced, how many emails were checked and found, a short error code if something failed). Lets our server check for new billing email. Removed when you disconnect.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Business data (expenses, team, payroll)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You and your team</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Business mode features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Household sharing</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You, and the people who join your household</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Combined household budgeting. <strong>Each member chooses individually how much they reveal — totals only, totals and categories, or full transactions. Nobody, including the household&#39;s owner, can raise another member&#39;s setting.</strong> Leaving a household revokes access immediately.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Crash reports</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">The app, when it crashes or hits an error</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Finding and fixing crashes. <strong>We send only the kind of error, where in the code it happened, the app version and the device model and OS, tied to your account&#39;s random ID. Never your email, name, amounts, notes, merchant or category names, anything you typed, or your IP address.</strong> Sent to Sentry, kept for at most 90 days.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Advisor profile, incl. health and allergies</td>\n<td data-label=\"Source\">You, optionally</td>\n<td data-label=\"Purpose\">Explaining why some spending categories cost you more than average. <strong>Stored only on your device — see below.</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"reading-your-inbox\">Reading your inbox</h2>\n<p>Connecting your email is optional. Latchet also finds subscriptions in your\nbank feed and in the statements you import.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-you-can-connect\">What you can connect</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gmail</strong>, with Google&#39;s read-only <code>gmail.readonly</code> permission.</li>\n<li><strong>Outlook</strong> (Outlook.com, Hotmail and Live — personal Microsoft accounts),\nwith Microsoft&#39;s read-only <code>Mail.Read</code> permission, plus <code>offline_access</code> so\nour server can check for new mail while you are not using the app, and\n<code>openid</code> and <code>email</code>, which tell us only which address you connected so we\ncan show it to you.</li>\n<li><strong>Forwarding</strong>, for any other provider (iCloud, Yahoo, GMX and others): you\ncreate a filter that forwards billing emails to your private Latchet\naddress, and we only ever see what you forward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>None of these lets Latchet send, delete, move, label or change your\nemail.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"what-we-read\">What we read</h3>\n<p>Reading happens on our server; your phone only receives the details we\nextract. The tokens Google and Microsoft give us to reach your inbox are\nencrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>On the first connection</strong>, our server asks your provider only for billing\nemail from the past 12 months: receipts, invoices, renewal notices, notices\nthat a free trial is ending, price changes and cancellations.</li>\n<li><strong>After that</strong>, it looks only at new mail.</li>\n<li><strong>Mail that does not look like a bill is not opened or kept.</strong> At most its\nsender and subject line are checked, in memory, to decide.</li>\n<li><strong>A billing email</strong> — including a PDF invoice attached to it — is read in\nmemory and discarded as soon as its details are extracted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-we-keep\">What we keep</h3>\n<p>From each billing email, only these details:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the merchant, and the sender&#39;s domain (e.g. <code>netflix.com</code>) — never the\nsender&#39;s address;</li>\n<li>the amount and currency, and the billing cycle (weekly, monthly, yearly…);</li>\n<li>the dates: when you were charged, when the email was sent, the next charge,\nthe end of a free trial;</li>\n<li>the previous price, when the email announces a change;</li>\n<li>what kind of email it was: receipt, renewal, trial ending, price change or\ncancellation;</li>\n<li>whether you were billed through Apple, Google Play or PayPal, so we can tell\nyou where to cancel;</li>\n<li>a short label (at most 60 characters) made from the subject line with every\nnumber removed, so an email we could not attribute still looks familiar;</li>\n<li>how confident the extraction was, and whether our rules or AI read it;</li>\n<li>a one-way hash of the email&#39;s message identifier, so the same email is never\ncounted twice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>We never keep the email body, its attachments, the subject line as written,\nor the address of anyone who sent or received it.</strong> The only email address we\nkeep is that of the inbox you connected, to show you which one it is.</p>\n<p>Your phone downloads these details and keeps a copy, because subscription\ndetection runs on the phone; that copy is removed when you sign out. The\nsubscriptions found this way join your subscription list, which syncs with your\naccount like everything else you track. <strong>An email never adds spending to your\ntransactions.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"rules-first-ai-only-as-a-fallback\">Rules first, AI only as a fallback</h3>\n<p>Our server reads billing email with rules. Only when AI reading is switched on\nfor our service, and only for a billing email the rules could not read, we send\nthat email&#39;s sender domain, subject and text to <strong>OpenAI</strong>, which returns the\nsame details; its answer passes the same checks as the rules&#39;. OpenAI does not\nuse data sent through its API to train its models, and keeps it for at most 30\ndays to monitor abuse.</p>\n<h3 id=\"disconnecting\">Disconnecting</h3>\n<p>You can disconnect an inbox in the app at any time. When you do:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gmail:</strong> we revoke Latchet&#39;s access with Google, so our token stops\nworking.</li>\n<li><strong>Outlook:</strong> we delete our tokens. Microsoft gives apps no way to withdraw\ntheir own access to a personal Microsoft account, so once our tokens are gone\nwe simply hold nothing that can reach your mailbox. You can also remove\nLatchet from your Microsoft account at\n<a href=\"https://account.live.com/consent/Manage\">account.live.com/consent/Manage</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Both:</strong> we delete the connection, and every detail we extracted from that\ninbox, from our server.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Subscriptions already in your list stay there until you remove them. You can\nalso remove Latchet&#39;s access from your Google account at any time at\n<a href=\"https://myaccount.google.com/permissions\">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>;\nthe app then shows the connection as needing to be reconnected.</p>\n<p><strong>Deleting your account</strong> revokes every connection first — Gmail with Google,\nyour bank with Plaid, and for Outlook our tokens are deleted as above — and then\ndeletes everything, as described under &quot;Your controls&quot;.</p>\n<h3 id=\"one-record-we-keep-after-you-leave\">One record we keep after you leave</h3>\n<p>Until Google has verified Latchet, Google lets it serve at most 100 Gmail\nusers over its whole lifetime, counting people who have since left. So\nthat we respect the same limit, we keep a record that an account once connected\nGmail or Outlook, and when. It holds a one-way hash of your account&#39;s random ID\n— no name, no email address — and once your account is deleted, nothing links\nit back to you.</p>\n<h3 id=\"googles-limited-use-requirements\">Google&#39;s Limited Use requirements</h3>\n<p>Latchet&#39;s use and transfer to any other app of information received from\nGoogle APIs will adhere to the\n<a href=\"https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy\">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,\nincluding the\n<a href=\"https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy#additional_requirements_for_specific_api_scopes\">Limited Use requirements</a>.</p>\n<p>In practice, for email from Gmail and Outlook alike:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>it is used only to show you your subscriptions, trials and price changes, and\nto remind you about them;</li>\n<li>it is never sold, never used for advertising, never used to decide\ncreditworthiness or lending, and never used to train AI models;</li>\n<li>no person at Latchet reads it, unless you ask us to look at a specific\nemail, or the law or the security of the service requires it;</li>\n<li>it is transferred only to provide this feature — to OpenAI as described\nabove, and to our hosting provider — or where the law requires it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-advisor-profile-stays-on-your-device\">The advisor profile stays on your device</h2>\n<p>If you fill in the advisor profile — household size, city, dietary\npreferences, and optionally allergies or health considerations — <strong>that\ninformation is stored only in the app&#39;s local database on your phone.</strong> It is\nnever uploaded to our servers, is not part of any cloud backup we hold, and is\nnot included when your transactions sync. If our backend were breached\ntomorrow, it would contain no health information from this app, because there\nis none there to take.</p>\n<p>Health and allergy information is a <strong>separate, explicit opt-in</strong>, kept apart\nfrom the rest of the profile. You can switch it off at any time, which erases\nthose entries; or delete the whole profile in one tap, which removes it from\nthe device with nothing recoverable. Deleting your account clears it too.</p>\n<p><strong>One thing this does not mean.</strong> When you ask the advisor a question, your\nprofile is included in the question sent to OpenAI, so that the answer can\naccount for your actual circumstances. That transmission is the point of the\nfeature. It is covered by the same API terms as the rest of our AI use —\ncontent sent for analysis is not used to train OpenAI&#39;s models — but it does\nleave your device at that moment, and you should know that before you fill the\nprofile in.</p>\n<p>The advisor is constrained, server-side, never to name specific products, to\nsay whether anything is safe for you to eat, or to give medical, nutritional\nor clinical advice. It uses this information to explain costs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-do-not-do\">What we do NOT do</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>We do not sell or share your data with advertisers or data brokers.</li>\n<li>We do not store your advisor profile, including any health or allergy\ninformation, on our servers.</li>\n<li>We do not read your email inbox unless <strong>you connect it</strong>, and then only\nbilling email, as described above. We can never send, delete or change your\nemail. Without a connection, only emails <strong>you choose to forward</strong> to your\nprivate Latchet address are processed.</li>\n<li>We do not store raw email content, sender addresses, subject lines, bank\ncredentials, or card numbers.</li>\n<li>We do not use anything from your email for advertising, to judge your\ncreditworthiness, or to train AI models.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"third-party-processors\">Third-party processors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plaid</strong> (bank connections) — <a href=\"https://plaid.com/legal\">plaid.com/legal</a></li>\n<li><strong>Google</strong> (Gmail) and <strong>Microsoft</strong> (Outlook) — only if you connect your\ninbox: they hold your email, and our server reads billing email from them as\ndescribed in &quot;Reading your inbox&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>OpenAI</strong> (receipt scans, statement import, the AI advisor, and — only when\nAI reading is on — billing emails our rules could not read) — content sent\nfor analysis is not used to train OpenAI models per API terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Railway</strong> (hosting) and <strong>Cloudflare</strong> (relays the emails you forward) —\ninfrastructure only.</li>\n<li><strong>Resend</strong> (email delivery) — sends the password-reset and email-verification messages to your address.</li>\n<li><strong>RevenueCat</strong> (subscriptions) — manages Pro: it receives your account&#39;s random ID and, from Apple or Google, your purchase receipts. It never sees your budget data.</li>\n<li><strong>Sentry</strong> (crash reporting) — receives the crash reports described above, with your IP address not stored. <a href=\"https://sentry.io/privacy/\">sentry.io/privacy</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"security\">Security</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>All traffic is encrypted in transit (HTTPS).</li>\n<li>Bank and inbox connection tokens (Plaid, Google, Microsoft) are encrypted at\nrest (AES-256-GCM).</li>\n<li>Passwords are hashed (PBKDF2-SHA256, 600k iterations — OWASP&#39;s current\nguidance); we never store them in plaintext.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-long-we-keep-things\">How long we keep things</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Data</th>\n<th>Kept for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Your transactions, accounts, budgets and business data</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">Until you delete them, or delete your account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Details extracted from billing emails (Gmail, Outlook or forwarded)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">90 days after we record them — or less: they go as soon as you disconnect that inbox or delete your account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Records of deleted items (the item&#39;s identifier — for a custom category, its name — and when)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">60 days, so your other phones remove the same item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Email-forwarding confirmation codes</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">24 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Expired sign-in sessions</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">7 days past expiry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Used one-time sign-in codes</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">24 hours past expiry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">Inbox connections (the inbox&#39;s address and the encrypted tokens)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">Until you disconnect the inbox or delete your account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">The copy of extracted email details on your phone</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">Until you sign out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-label=\"Data\">The record that an account once connected Gmail or Outlook (a one-way hash, no name or email address)</td>\n<td data-label=\"Kept for\">Kept after your account is deleted, because Google&#39;s limit counts every user an unverified app has ever had</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The rows with a fixed period are removed automatically by a daily job. Your own\nledger is never expired — it is yours until you remove it.</p>\n<p>Subscriptions detected from an email are part of your subscription list and are\nnot affected when the email details expire or when you disconnect the inbox.</p>\n<h2 id=\"your-controls\">Your controls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Disconnect</strong> a bank or an inbox at any time in the app. Plaid and Google\ntokens are revoked with the provider and removed; Outlook tokens are removed\n(Microsoft offers apps no way to revoke them for personal accounts — see\n&quot;Disconnecting&quot; above). Everything extracted from a disconnected inbox is\ndeleted.</li>\n<li><strong>Reset All Activity</strong> (Settings) permanently deletes your transactions and\nbalances.</li>\n<li><strong>Account deletion</strong>: Settings → Delete Account removes your account and\neverything in it — transactions, accounts, budgets, bill reminders, recurring\nrules, achievements, and any business you own. Bank and inbox connections are\nrevoked with the provider first, so they stop sharing data with us (for\nOutlook, we delete our tokens, as above). Deletion is immediate and\nirreversible; we keep no copy — only the anonymous record described under\n&quot;One record we keep after you leave&quot; — and cannot restore it.\nIf a provider is unreachable at that moment we still delete your account, and\nthe app tells you which connections to revoke yourself.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"this-website\">This website</h2>\n<p>Latchet&#39;s website (its home page, this policy and the terms of service) sets\nno cookies, runs no scripts or analytics, and loads nothing from other sites.\nLike the app&#39;s own traffic, requests to it pass through our hosting provider,\nRailway.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contact\">Contact</h2>\n<p>Questions: <a href=\"mailto:support@latchet.app\">support@latchet.app</a></p>\n<p>You do not need to contact us to delete your account — Settings → Delete Account\ndoes it in full.</p>",
  "title": "Privacy Policy",
  "updated": "October 14, 2026"
} as const;

